WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.640
- Do you remember how inescapable pop punk

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:04.480
was in the early 2000's?

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:06.670
blink-182 was all over MTV.

00:00:06.670 --> 00:00:08.230
The band's drummer, Travis Barker,

00:00:08.230 --> 00:00:09.910
even had a reality TV show.

00:00:09.910 --> 00:00:11.560
Pete Wentz, of Fall Out Boy,

00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:13.930
was frequently on the cover of J-14.

00:00:13.930 --> 00:00:17.390
Simple Plan even made a cameo
in an Olsen twins movie.

00:00:17.390 --> 00:00:20.077
Eventually, fans and bands grew up

00:00:20.077 --> 00:00:23.970
and pop punk faded from the
limelight in the early 2010s.

00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:27.050
- Fast forward to today, pop punk is back

00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:29.340
and Gen Z is reviving the scene.

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:30.731
Musicians like Willow,

00:00:30.731 --> 00:00:33.740
Meet Me @ The Altar, Pinkshift,

00:00:33.740 --> 00:00:36.180
And Kenny Hoopla have taken the torch.

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:38.143
Even Gen Z artists outside the genre

00:00:38.143 --> 00:00:41.498
like Olivia Rodrigo and
The Kid Laroi are borrowing

00:00:41.498 --> 00:00:45.350
from pop punk, resulting
in viral chart toppers.

00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:47.040
- In this video, we're gonna dig

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:50.410
into just how Gen Z is
leading a pop punk revival

00:00:50.410 --> 00:00:52.320
and make sure you stay till the end,

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:55.531
where we try to create our
own pop punk inspired tune.

00:00:55.531 --> 00:00:59.031
(pop punk inspired music)

00:01:00.510 --> 00:01:03.410
- Okay. So what does pop punk even mean?

00:01:03.410 --> 00:01:06.370
Well, it's basically an offshoot
of punk that drifts away

00:01:06.370 --> 00:01:10.080
from the heavy and aggressive
to a melodic and pop sound.

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:12.770
Some say The Ramones were
the first pop punk band,

00:01:12.770 --> 00:01:15.310
but the Descendants became
the blueprint for pop punk

00:01:15.310 --> 00:01:19.050
in 1982, with their LP,
Milo Goes To College.

00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:22.350
("Suburban Home" by Descendents)

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:25.470
Descendants' drummer Bill
Stevenson cited his influences

00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:29.560
as the Kinks, The Seeds, The
Ramones, and the early Beatles.

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:32.530
Without The Descendants,
bands like blink-182,

00:01:32.530 --> 00:01:35.050
who pushed pop punk into the mainstream,

00:01:35.050 --> 00:01:36.370
may have never existed.

00:01:36.370 --> 00:01:39.630
In 2016, Mark Hoppus of blink-182 said,

00:01:39.630 --> 00:01:41.470
"The first song of The Descendants

00:01:41.470 --> 00:01:44.270
I heard was "Silly Girl",
and it blew my mind.

00:01:44.270 --> 00:01:46.870
That just changed the
trajectory of my life forever."

00:01:46.870 --> 00:01:49.490
- When pop punk first
emerged in the late 80's

00:01:49.490 --> 00:01:51.700
it wasn't an immediate commercial success.

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:55.700
Then in 1994, that changed
when Green Day became

00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:58.660
an overnight sensation
with their album Dookie,

00:01:58.660 --> 00:02:01.058
which went on to win a Grammy in 1995.

00:02:01.058 --> 00:02:05.080
("Basket Case" by Green Day)

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:09.570
- Following Green Day, bands
like blink-182, Newfound Glory,

00:02:09.570 --> 00:02:11.844
and Sum 41 began to take over the radio.

00:02:11.844 --> 00:02:15.540
blink-182 became one of
pop punk's biggest names

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:19.750
after the success of their 1999
album "Enema of the State".

00:02:19.750 --> 00:02:21.530
The songs had a coming of age feel,

00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:23.700
fast and driving drums were paired

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:26.208
with a mix of goofy,
but melancholic lyrics.

00:02:26.208 --> 00:02:30.071
("All the Small Things" by blink-182)

00:02:30.071 --> 00:02:32.687
♪ All the small things ♪
- Yeah?

00:02:32.687 --> 00:02:36.870
- I was kind of like a closeted
fan of pop punk, for sure.

00:02:36.870 --> 00:02:40.540
- When I was a teenager, pop punk was like

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:42.960
the cool music to listen to.

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:44.810
As the mid 2000's approached,

00:02:44.810 --> 00:02:48.590
mainstream radio started
to shift from rock and pop

00:02:48.590 --> 00:02:52.721
toward R&amp;B and hip hop, with
artists like Kanye and Rihanna.

00:02:52.721 --> 00:02:56.090
("SOS" by Rihanna)

00:02:56.090 --> 00:02:59.460
- During this shift, a new
generation of pop punk bands

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:01.240
found an audience with the youth.

00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:03.250
Artists like Paramore, Fall Out Boy,

00:03:03.250 --> 00:03:06.080
My Chemical Romance,
and Panic! at the Disco.

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:08.660
These bands signaled a shift
in the sound of pop punk

00:03:08.660 --> 00:03:11.930
as they took influence from
the emo and scene subcultures.

00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:14.420
Their new moodier version of the genre

00:03:14.420 --> 00:03:17.243
started to blur the
lines defining pop punk.

00:03:17.243 --> 00:03:19.520
Were these bands were pop punk?

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:21.020
Were they emo pop?

00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:23.980
While there's some debate
on where these bands fit in,

00:03:23.980 --> 00:03:25.910
these artists use characteristics

00:03:25.910 --> 00:03:27.982
from both genres in their music.

00:03:27.982 --> 00:03:32.150
("crushcrushcrush" by Paramore)

00:03:32.150 --> 00:03:35.160
- During this time, many of
these successful pop punk

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:38.650
and emo pop bands performed
at the Vans Warped Tour.

00:03:38.650 --> 00:03:41.670
Warped Tour began in 1995 and started

00:03:41.670 --> 00:03:43.400
as a skate punk music tour

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:45.270
with lots of third wave Ska musicians

00:03:45.270 --> 00:03:46.690
featured in the lineup.

00:03:46.690 --> 00:03:49.620
The tour soon morphed into
a predominantly pop punk

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:51.240
and metal core focused tour.

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:55.220
During the 2010s, pop punk's
popularity began to fade,

00:03:55.220 --> 00:03:58.530
possibly because mainstream
radio began to favor hip hop,

00:03:58.530 --> 00:04:00.400
possibly because the teens that listened

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:02.230
to the genre began to grow up.

00:04:02.230 --> 00:04:05.550
Or maybe the bands found it
difficult to adapt to the times.

00:04:05.550 --> 00:04:08.190
Hayley Williams said the
scene during the early 2000s

00:04:08.190 --> 00:04:11.460
was "A lot of internalized sexism,

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:13.010
and even when you were lucky enough to

00:04:13.010 --> 00:04:15.389
meet other bands who
were kind and respectful,

00:04:15.389 --> 00:04:17.620
there was other that wasn't."

00:04:17.620 --> 00:04:19.590
The genre had a diversity problem.

00:04:19.590 --> 00:04:23.120
Black artists and women
musicians were rare in the scene.

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:26.171
Travie McCoy, the front
man of Gym Class Heroes,

00:04:26.171 --> 00:04:28.330
said he struggled with finding acceptance

00:04:28.330 --> 00:04:30.067
within the pop punk community.

00:04:30.067 --> 00:04:31.860
"I was definitely a sore thumb

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:33.810
at a lot of shows in the early days."

00:04:33.810 --> 00:04:35.780
Fefe Dobson, a pop punk singer,

00:04:35.780 --> 00:04:38.100
said she dealt with companies
attempting to rebrand her

00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:40.480
as an R&amp;B artist, which she refused.

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:42.440
Dobson showed young black
girls that they can be

00:04:42.440 --> 00:04:45.251
angsty, angry, and alternative too.

00:04:45.251 --> 00:04:49.251
("Take Me Away" by Fefe Dobson)

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:52.531
- What was that experience like?

00:04:52.531 --> 00:04:54.340
Just going back a little bit,

00:04:54.340 --> 00:04:57.790
how did you feel that the
label tried to mold you?

00:04:57.790 --> 00:04:59.360
- The one thing that
actually was the red flag,

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:01.380
was, I think they thought
it was a compliment but,

00:05:01.380 --> 00:05:04.650
they coined me as Brandy Spears.

00:05:04.650 --> 00:05:06.670
And that really bothered me,

00:05:06.670 --> 00:05:09.150
because first of all, I'm not, you know

00:05:09.150 --> 00:05:11.920
I love Brandy, love Brandy,
but I'm not, I'm not them.

00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:14.140
So basically it's like, well, she's Brandy

00:05:14.140 --> 00:05:17.278
because she's black but she
has got a poppy white voice,

00:05:17.278 --> 00:05:19.750
so she's Britney.

00:05:19.750 --> 00:05:21.280
And that's how they were selling me

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:24.990
so that as young as I was, I was about 15.

00:05:24.990 --> 00:05:29.990
I knew that felt weird. That
felt weird to already be put

00:05:30.150 --> 00:05:33.150
in a box and put in a category and coined

00:05:33.150 --> 00:05:35.960
with a different name right off the bat.

00:05:35.960 --> 00:05:38.130
- Especially a box that is just so not

00:05:38.130 --> 00:05:40.040
representative of yourself.

00:05:40.040 --> 00:05:43.280
Do you feel that pop rock and pop punk,

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:45.320
needed to be reinvented?

00:05:45.320 --> 00:05:47.770
- I'm happy pop punk is back or pop rock.

00:05:47.770 --> 00:05:51.880
I would say both genres, but
I would also say that like,

00:05:51.880 --> 00:05:54.190
hell yeah. Why not?

00:05:54.190 --> 00:05:57.251
You know, I think that we
also went through a crazy time

00:05:57.251 --> 00:06:00.020
in the last few years.

00:06:00.020 --> 00:06:03.080
We need young people and,
and anybody to come out

00:06:03.080 --> 00:06:05.938
and be honest and shout how they feel and

00:06:05.938 --> 00:06:10.430
and be unapologetic because
we've gone through too much.

00:06:10.430 --> 00:06:14.040
And we were hit with so much
in the last three years that

00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:19.040
you know, we had to come back musically.

00:06:19.459 --> 00:06:24.290
With you know, just honest, raw emotion.

00:06:24.290 --> 00:06:27.880
- During the pandemic, TikTok
began introducing pop punk

00:06:27.880 --> 00:06:31.000
to a new generation and
also brought nostalgia

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:33.010
to the genre's long term fans.

00:06:33.010 --> 00:06:35.251
Many 2000s pop punk classics from bands

00:06:35.251 --> 00:06:38.940
like All Time Low and
Paramore went viral on the app

00:06:38.940 --> 00:06:42.451
with videos using their songs,
getting up to 3 million likes

00:06:42.451 --> 00:06:46.586
("Dear Maria, Count Me
In" by All Time Low)

00:06:46.586 --> 00:06:47.950
- With its growing community,

00:06:47.950 --> 00:06:51.200
we're seeing more diversity
and TikTokers are utilizing

00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:56.000
hashtags like #blackalt and #altPOC,

00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:57.860
which have gained millions of views.

00:06:57.860 --> 00:07:01.410
These include bands like
De'Wayne, Magnolia Park,

00:07:01.410 --> 00:07:05.305
Kenny Hoopla, Cherym,
and Meet Me @ the Altar.

00:07:05.305 --> 00:07:09.270
("Brighter Days (Are Before
Us)" by Meet Me @ the Altar)

00:07:09.270 --> 00:07:11.330
- No one found out about us at all

00:07:11.330 --> 00:07:15.050
for like four or five years,
because we were over here

00:07:15.050 --> 00:07:18.140
like putting our music
on Spotify and all that.

00:07:18.140 --> 00:07:20.190
We were kind of just like
screaming, like, "Hey, we exist!"

00:07:20.190 --> 00:07:21.660
But no one was hearing it.

00:07:21.660 --> 00:07:24.400
So I'm definitely sure that
there's other bands out there

00:07:24.400 --> 00:07:27.220
that are like that,
which I'm glad our band

00:07:27.220 --> 00:07:29.540
getting recognition is
gonna help them, like

00:07:29.540 --> 00:07:32.220
know that they have a space
where they can reach out

00:07:32.220 --> 00:07:34.210
and people will actually hear them.

00:07:34.210 --> 00:07:37.030
- All these bands are in
their own little bubble

00:07:37.030 --> 00:07:38.760
of pop punk. If you look from the outside,

00:07:38.760 --> 00:07:41.747
it's all these white guys talking about

00:07:41.747 --> 00:07:43.760
their girlfriends or ex-girlfriends

00:07:43.760 --> 00:07:45.660
or girlfriends they never got, whatever.

00:07:45.660 --> 00:07:47.290
And then it's just all the same stuff.

00:07:47.290 --> 00:07:49.990
And then all of these
girls who are minorities

00:07:49.990 --> 00:07:51.910
are also in the bubble,
but you don't see them

00:07:51.910 --> 00:07:53.700
because there's not very many of them.

00:07:53.700 --> 00:07:56.390
It's a community that like,
we can't really relate

00:07:56.390 --> 00:07:59.160
to sometimes. So that's
why we try to make our own

00:07:59.160 --> 00:08:01.848
and I think that's something
that we do as a band.

00:08:01.848 --> 00:08:04.681
- Ladies and ladies. No gentlemen.

00:08:06.196 --> 00:08:07.946
Show me what you got.

00:08:11.000 --> 00:08:12.920
- Yeah, I think it's, it's awesome,

00:08:12.920 --> 00:08:15.170
but also frustrating
to be in that position

00:08:15.170 --> 00:08:17.640
of being the first. People are saying

00:08:17.640 --> 00:08:20.640
that Gen Z is reinventing pop punk.

00:08:20.640 --> 00:08:22.960
And do you feel like that's the case?

00:08:22.960 --> 00:08:26.450
- It's so strange to see how much,

00:08:26.450 --> 00:08:28.787
like pop punk's not dead, that playlist

00:08:28.787 --> 00:08:32.087
on Spotify, has changed,
because originally

00:08:32.087 --> 00:08:36.380
that was only bands, only bands up there.

00:08:36.380 --> 00:08:40.100
But now like more than
half of it is solo artists

00:08:40.100 --> 00:08:42.730
who just have pop punk
instrumentals behind it.

00:08:42.730 --> 00:08:45.230
So it's like, it's definitely changing

00:08:45.230 --> 00:08:47.568
and it's so weird to see it change

00:08:47.568 --> 00:08:50.659
so drastically over the last couple years.

00:08:50.659 --> 00:08:52.118
- What are some positive reinventions

00:08:52.118 --> 00:08:53.910
that you're seeing happen?

00:08:53.910 --> 00:08:58.560
- I do like that some
artists are starting to sound

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:00.660
more like the early 2000s.

00:09:00.660 --> 00:09:03.306
Like any Amanda Binds
movie, like soundtrack.

00:09:03.306 --> 00:09:05.147
- Yes!

00:09:05.147 --> 00:09:07.640
- That, that kinda, that
kinda sound is coming back

00:09:07.640 --> 00:09:11.660
which is always so nostalgic
and sounds so good.

00:09:11.660 --> 00:09:14.107
- Gen Z's Disney upbringing
has also been a huge

00:09:14.107 --> 00:09:16.810
influence in the revival today.

00:09:16.810 --> 00:09:19.800
They're influenced by Disney
stars like Demi Levato

00:09:19.800 --> 00:09:22.427
and Miley Cyrus, who
released pop rock albums

00:09:22.427 --> 00:09:24.307
early on in their careers.

00:09:24.307 --> 00:09:27.974
("7 Things" by Miley Cyrus)

00:09:29.030 --> 00:09:31.968
- While pop rock isn't
the same as pop punk,

00:09:31.968 --> 00:09:34.450
Disney pop rock was many young girls'

00:09:34.450 --> 00:09:36.850
first introduction to alternative rock.

00:09:36.850 --> 00:09:39.330
And today we're seeing many young women

00:09:39.330 --> 00:09:41.780
involved in the revival of pop punk.

00:09:41.780 --> 00:09:43.700
Can you name the songs that you wrote

00:09:43.700 --> 00:09:46.000
for these big Disney stars

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:49.300
and how did Disney get
their hands on the songs?

00:09:49.300 --> 00:09:51.790
- For Miley it was "Start All Over".

00:09:51.790 --> 00:09:53.287
For Selena, it was a song called

00:09:53.287 --> 00:09:57.488
"As a Blonde", "Round and
Round" was another one.

00:09:57.488 --> 00:09:59.210
- Such a good song.

00:09:59.210 --> 00:10:01.600
- Well, I had an album called Sunday Love

00:10:01.600 --> 00:10:05.078
that I wrote in LA. There
were songs that were

00:10:05.078 --> 00:10:06.950
on that album, that are on the album,

00:10:06.950 --> 00:10:08.130
you could hear my version of them,

00:10:08.130 --> 00:10:10.928
that got picked up by these girls.

00:10:10.928 --> 00:10:14.867
("Start All Over" by Miley Cyrus)

00:10:14.867 --> 00:10:16.840
- That Disney influence
still exists today.

00:10:16.840 --> 00:10:20.299
Ex Disney star Olivia
Rodrigo released a pop punk

00:10:20.299 --> 00:10:23.648
inspired single that
dominated charts in 2021.

00:10:23.648 --> 00:10:27.560
("Good 4 U" by Olivia Rodrigo)

00:10:27.560 --> 00:10:29.820
- Another young star in
the pop punk revival is

00:10:29.820 --> 00:10:31.757
Willow, who released her dance track

00:10:31.757 --> 00:10:33.486
"Whip My Hair" back in 2010.

00:10:33.486 --> 00:10:37.069
("Whip My Hair" by Willow)

00:10:38.008 --> 00:10:40.580
- Willow's newest track, Transparent Soul,

00:10:40.580 --> 00:10:43.090
has blown up on TikTok and features

00:10:43.090 --> 00:10:44.664
a heavy pop punk aesthetic.

00:10:44.664 --> 00:10:49.664
("Transparent Soul" by
Willow feat. Travis Barker)

00:10:50.560 --> 00:10:53.840
Pop punk has also influenced
many of today's rap stars.

00:10:53.840 --> 00:10:56.580
Artists like Lil Uzi Vert and Juice Wrld

00:10:56.580 --> 00:10:59.700
have cited pop punk bands
as major inspirations.

00:10:59.700 --> 00:11:02.130
Gen Z has shown the industry
that they still want

00:11:02.130 --> 00:11:04.625
their pop punk loud and
catchy, but for the leaders

00:11:04.625 --> 00:11:08.750
of the scene to reflect the
diverse society we live in.

00:11:08.750 --> 00:11:10.187
Okay. So wait, our, the song.

00:11:10.187 --> 00:11:11.020
- Yes. Let's talk about the song

00:11:12.340 --> 00:11:17.340
- I peep the guitar progression
is very typical of pop punk.

00:11:18.240 --> 00:11:20.476
- Yeah. I chose a very
hooky like very basic

00:11:20.476 --> 00:11:23.838
chord progression and it was fun.

00:11:23.838 --> 00:11:25.196
Nice.

00:11:25.196 --> 00:11:26.029
Okay.

00:11:26.029 --> 00:11:27.928
- I'm going to play bass on it.

00:11:27.928 --> 00:11:32.267
I gotta record some drums on
it and it's gonna be such a,

00:11:32.267 --> 00:11:34.260
it's gonna be a real small kick.

00:11:39.000 --> 00:11:41.160
- When you sing, just let yourself go.

00:11:41.160 --> 00:11:43.860
Like, you know, all that
angst that you have,

00:11:43.860 --> 00:11:46.086
channel that and belt.

00:11:46.086 --> 00:11:51.086
♪ You're never growing up at all ♪

00:11:51.315 --> 00:11:53.475
♪ In a sad way, you're absurd ♪

00:11:53.475 --> 00:11:56.825
♪ It's pathetic, you're the worst ♪

00:11:56.825 --> 00:12:01.280
♪ You're never growing up at all ♪

00:12:01.280 --> 00:12:04.140
- The concept I'm working with is

00:12:04.140 --> 00:12:07.985
calling out the hypocrisy
of people, of society.

00:12:07.985 --> 00:12:10.136
♪ You are lying to yourself ♪

00:12:10.136 --> 00:12:14.500
♪ Never find your way out of this ♪

00:12:14.500 --> 00:12:16.810
- As always, thanks for watching.

00:12:16.810 --> 00:12:19.580
We want to hear what your
favorite pop punk tracks are.

00:12:19.580 --> 00:12:22.440
If you'd like you can submit
a video of you telling us

00:12:22.440 --> 00:12:24.700
your favorite songs at the
link in our description.

00:12:24.700 --> 00:12:26.950
Here are your submissions
from our last video,

00:12:26.950 --> 00:12:27.783
where we asked you

00:12:27.783 --> 00:12:29.777
what your favorite backing
vocals in a song are.

00:12:29.777 --> 00:12:32.520
- One of the best backing
vocals, that have been

00:12:32.520 --> 00:12:34.100
a very very big inspiration to me,

00:12:34.100 --> 00:12:35.830
has been the D'Angelo Voodoo album.

